That changes the scope in a big way—and it makes complete sense. If the goal is running existing, un-recompiled Linux x86/x86_64 binaries directly on Haiku without source access, relying on C-library dynamic linking won't cut it (especially for Go, Rust, or statically linked binaries that issue syscall / sysenter directly).

To intercept raw x86 syscalls at the instruction level on Haiku without recompiling the target binaries, there are three primary architectural paths:

Option 1: Kernel-Level Syscall Vectoring (The FreeBSD linux.ko Path)
This is the cleanest and highest-performance method. When a Linux ELF binary runs, the Haiku kernel handles its execution state.

How it works:

The Haiku ELF loader identifies the binary as a Linux executable (via ELF header OS ABI tag or header flags).

When the thread executes syscall (or sysenter / int 0x80), the CPU hardware trap vectors straight into kernel space.

Instead of routing the syscall number to Haiku's native dispatch table, the kernel checks the process flag and routes it to a Linux Syscall Dispatch Table (implemented as a Haiku kernel module).

The dispatch table maps the Linux syscall number (e.g., Linux sys_write = 1) directly to Haiku kernel primitives or Haiku VFS routines, translating structure layouts in-place.

Pros: Zero-copy execution, near 100% native performance, handles static binaries transparently.

Cons: Requires adding a secondary syscall dispatch path inside Haiku's kernel manager (src/system/kernel/arch/x86).

Option 2: Userland Interception via ptrace / Debugger API (The PRoot Path)
If you want to keep the entire implementation strictly in userland without patching Haiku's kernel source code, you can use process tracing.

How it works:

A parent loader process launches the Linux binary using Haiku’s debugger() / ptrace() equivalent (install_team_debugger).

The tracer sets the TRacing_SYSCALL flag to intercept every entry and exit of a system call.

When the guest binary hits syscall, the kernel pauses the thread and notifies the tracer process.

The tracer inspects the CPU registers (RAX/EAX for syscall number, RDI/RSI/RDX for args), executes the equivalent Haiku POSIX call natively, writes the result back to RAX, and resumes the thread.

Pros: 100% userland module; zero modifications needed to the core Haiku kernel.

Cons: High context-switching overhead. Every single Linux syscall triggers context switches between the app, kernel, and tracer process. Fine for CLI tools, slow for high-I/O applications.

Option 3: Userland Binary Rewriting / Binary Translation
A hybrid approach that modifies the x86 code segments in memory at load time.

How it works:

A custom userland loader maps the Linux ELF into memory.

It scans the executable .text sections for raw opcode sequences corresponding to syscall (0F 05), sysenter (0F 34), or int 0x80 (CD 80).

It replaces those opcode bytes with a call (CALL rel32) or jump (JMP) to a trampoline function inside a loaded liblinux_shim.so.

The trampoline function reads the register state, translates the arguments, calls native Haiku libroot.so functions, updates registers, and returns control to the guest binary.

Pros: Runs entirely in userland at near-native speed without ptrace context-switch overhead.

Cons: Variable-length x86 instruction decoding is tricky (you have to ensure you don't overwrite instruction boundaries or jump targets), and dynamically generated code (JITs) won't be caught during initial load-time scanning.

Structural Mappings Needed
Regardless of which interception mechanism you choose, the translation engine needs to handle three core structural differences:

Syscall Table Translation: Mapping Linux x86_64 ABI register assignments (RAX = call, RDI, RSI, RDX, R10, R8, R9) to Haiku’s internal kernel parameter passing format.

struct Memory Layout Alignment: Linux struct stat, struct timeval, and socket structures (struct sockaddr) have different field offsets, sizes, and flag bitmasks compared to Haiku. The translator must unpack the Linux struct, populate the Haiku equivalent, call Haiku, and repack the result into the Linux memory buffer.

Pseudo-Filesystem Emulation: Intercepting path-based syscalls (openat, statat) referencing /proc/self/cmdline, /proc/meminfo, or /sys/... and returning synthesized memory buffers so Linux runtimes don't panic on boot.

