Step 1: Flagging the Linux Execution Thread
When execve() loads an ELF binary, Haiku’s kernel loader parses the headers. Linux ELFs typically contain an .note.ABI-tag section or distinct ELF OS ABI branding in the header (ELFOSABI_LINUX / 0x03).

Inside your module’s team/thread initialization hook:

Mark the thread in Haiku's struct Thread with an OS flag (e.g., THREAD_FLAG_ABI_LINUX).

Set up a thread-local Linux environment block (to track Linux-specific signal masks, thread IDs, and process group structures).

Step 2: The Kernel Vector & Dispatch Table
On x86_64 Linux, system calls pass parameters via registers:

RAX: Syscall Number

RDI, RSI, RDX, R10, R8, R9: Arguments 1 through 6

Inside the Linux kernel module, you define the translation dispatch vector table:

C
// linux_syscalls.h
typedef int64 (*linux_syscall_func)(uint64 arg1, uint64 arg2, uint64 arg3, 
                                    uint64 arg4, uint64 arg5, uint64 arg6);

// Linux x86_64 Syscall Numbers
#define LINUX_SYS_READ    0
#define LINUX_SYS_WRITE   1
#define LINUX_SYS_OPEN    2
#define LINUX_SYS_CLOSE   3
#define LINUX_SYS_STAT    4
#define LINUX_SYS_FSTAT   5

extern linux_syscall_func gLinuxSyscallTable[500];
Inside the main dispatch function:

C
// linux_dispatch.cpp
int64
linux_syscall_dispatcher(struct iframe* frame)
{
    uint64 syscall_num = frame->rax;

    if (syscall_num >= MAX_LINUX_SYSCALLS || gLinuxSyscallTable[syscall_num] == NULL) {
        dprintf("linux_compat: Unimplemented Linux Syscall %" B_PRIu64 "\n", syscall_num);
        return -ENOSYS; // Linux ENOSYS
    }

    // Pass x86_64 ABI registers from saved interrupt frame
    return gLinuxSyscallTable[syscall_num](
        frame->rdi, frame->rsi, frame->rdx,
        frame->r10, frame->r8,  frame->r9
    );
}
Step 3: Translating Call Primitives & Structure Layouts
This is where the real work happens. Because field offsets and flag bitmasks differ between Linux and Haiku, functions cannot simply pass pointers through directly.

Example 1: sys_write (Direct Mapping)
Some calls map almost 1:1 with Haiku kernel primitives (sys_write_port or VFS writes):

C
int64 
linux_sys_write(uint64 fd, uint64 user_buf, uint64 count)
{
    // Validate userland pointer safety using Haiku kernel memory checks
    if (!IS_USER_ADDRESS(user_buf))
        return -EFAULT;

    // Delegate directly to Haiku VFS layer
    ssize_t bytes_written = _kern_write(fd, -1, (const void*)user_buf, count);
    
    if (bytes_written < 0)
        return haiku_to_linux_error(bytes_written);

    return bytes_written;
}
Example 2: sys_stat (Structure Unpacking/Repacking)
Linux struct stat has different field alignments and sizes than Haiku's struct stat.

C
int64
linux_sys_stat(const char* user_path, struct linux_stat* user_linux_stat)
{
    char path[PATH_MAX];
    struct stat haiku_st;

    // 1. Copy path from userland to kernel
    if (user_strlcpy(path, user_path, sizeof(path)) < 0)
        return -EFAULT;

    // 2. Perform Haiku VFS call
    status_t status = _kern_read_stat(-1, path, true, &haiku_st, sizeof(haiku_st));
    if (status != B_OK)
        return haiku_to_linux_error(status);

    // 3. Populate the Linux-formatted stat struct
    struct linux_stat lstat;
    memset(&lstat, 0, sizeof(lstat));
    lstat.st_dev     = haiku_st.st_dev;
    lstat.st_ino     = haiku_st.st_ino;
    lstat.st_mode    = haiku_to_linux_mode(haiku_st.st_mode);
    lstat.st_nlink   = haiku_st.st_nlink;
    lstat.st_uid     = haiku_st.st_uid;
    lstat.st_gid     = haiku_st.st_gid;
    lstat.st_size    = haiku_st.st_size;
    lstat.st_atime   = haiku_st.st_atime;
    lstat.st_mtime   = haiku_st.st_mtime;
    lstat.st_ctime   = haiku_st.st_ctime;

    // 4. Copy back to userland memory buffer
    if (user_memcpy(user_linux_stat, &lstat, sizeof(lstat)) != B_OK)
        return -EFAULT;

    return 0;
}
