The Cost of the Custom Kernel Route
The Driver Vacuum: Hardware vendors write drivers for Linux and Windows. By maintaining a custom kernel architecture, Haiku is constantly forced to write wrapper glue layers (like their FreeBSD network stack shim or OpenBSD USB drivers) just to get basic Wi-Fi, Ethernet, and audio working.

Kernel Stability vs. API Focus: A massive amount of contributor bandwidth over the last 20+ years went into fixing VM panics, scheduler deadlocks, and hardware edge cases in the NewOS descendant kernel—time that wasn't being spent on application tooling, browser engines, or user-facing APIs.

Missing POSIX/Linux Parity: Because Linux evolved into the de facto standard target for modern open-source software, Haiku's native POSIX implementation is constantly playing catch-up to build dependencies for modern runtimes (Go, Rust, Node, Python libraries).

